<UserControl x:Class="ROROROblox.App.Tray.AlertBalloon"
             x:ClassModifier="internal"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Name="Root">

    <!-- THE BALLOON WE DRAW (v1.33 item 4). Shown through TaskbarIcon.ShowCustomBalloon, which is
         a WPF Popup positioned near the tray icon — not the shell balloon.

         WHY NOT THE SHELL BALLOON. ShowBalloonTip always plays the system notification sound.
         BalloonFlags.NoSound exists in Hardcodet.NotifyIcon.Wpf 2.0.1 but only behind a non-public
         overload (verified by reflection 2026-10-05), so there is no supported way to ask for a
         silent one. A balloon that draws no OS chrome plays no OS sound, which is the point: the
         sound becomes a setting rather than something Windows decides (item 5 owns that; this
         control deliberately plays nothing).

         WHAT WE GIVE UP, recorded rather than discovered later: no Action Center entry (the shell
         balloon had none either) and quiet-hours behaviour is ours now — nothing here consults
         Focus Assist. Both are in the PRD's non-goals.

         DYNAMICRESOURCE ONLY, EVERYWHERE. ThemeService.ApplySlot REPLACES each brush instance on a
         theme change rather than mutating the held brush, so a StaticResource would render
         correctly on first paint and then never repaint again. See App.xaml's note on the brush
         block. AlertBalloonTests builds this control under each of the four built-in themes and
         reads back the brush every named slot actually resolved to, plus a narrower pixel pass
         proving it paints at all — so a slot that stops resolving fails the suite rather than the
         eye. Measured, not reasoned: a first version of that test read the ratio off the bitmap and
         stayed GREEN with the body text mutated to the divider hairline, because the most-contrasty
         pixel was this border.

         THE SURFACE IS THE BUTTON. One click target, the whole balloon, because the clickable
         thing IS the notification — there is no second action to offer. It takes
         InteractiveEdgeBrush (WCAG 1.4.11's 3:1) rather than the card hairline for a reason the
         app's other surfaces do not have: this one floats over whatever the user happens to have
         on screen, so a faint edge against an unknown background is no edge at all.

         THE ONLY AFFORDANCE IS THE CURSOR, set from the code-behind when an account id arrives —
         no "click to show this account" line, because a new displayed string is six translations
         (LocKeyParityFenceTests holds parity across en/fr/de/ru/pt-BR/pl/es) and inventing them is
         worse than the shell balloon's own silence on the same question. A hint belongs with
         item 6's copy pass, with real translations, if it is wanted at all. -->

    <Border x:Name="Surface"
            Background="{DynamicResource RowBgBrush}"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}"
            BorderThickness="1"
            CornerRadius="6"
            Padding="14,12,14,12"
            MinWidth="240"
            MaxWidth="340"
            MouseLeftButtonUp="OnSurfaceClicked"
            AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Title, ElementName=Root}"
            AutomationProperties.HelpText="{Binding Body, ElementName=Root}"
            AutomationProperties.LiveSetting="Polite">

        <StackPanel>
            <!-- Heading step, not Display: this sits in a 340px popup, not a page hero. -->
            <TextBlock x:Name="TitleText"
                       Text="{Binding Title, ElementName=Root}"
                       FontSize="{DynamicResource HeadingFontSize}"
                       FontWeight="SemiBold"
                       Foreground="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap" />

            <!-- The body is prose, so it keeps the prose token. ContrastPairGateTests measures
                 MutedText on RowBg unconditionally as its most common pairing, so this pair is
                 already watched in every built-in theme. -->
            <TextBlock x:Name="BodyText"
                       Text="{Binding Body, ElementName=Root}"
                       FontSize="{DynamicResource BodyFontSize}"
                       Foreground="{DynamicResource MutedTextBrush}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Margin="0,4,0,0" />
        </StackPanel>
    </Border>
</UserControl>
